1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,684
Your build runs on your laptop, and fails on the server, with a four oh one. Unauthorized.

2
00:00:06,234 --> 00:00:09,957
Your company keeps its libraries in a private repository.

3
00:00:09,957 --> 00:00:11,067
It needs a login.

4
00:00:11,067 --> 00:00:13,679
And that login must never end up in git.

5
00:00:14,229 --> 00:00:16,202
Today you learn what a mirror is,

6
00:00:16,202 --> 00:00:18,234
where Maven looks for credentials,

7
00:00:18,234 --> 00:00:20,565
and how to hand over a password safely.

8
00:00:21,115 --> 00:00:22,566
Then you set it up yourself.

9
00:00:23,116 --> 00:00:24,262
One honest note.

10
00:00:24,262 --> 00:00:29,417
The company repository in this lesson is a small local server I started,

11
00:00:29,417 --> 00:00:30,348
with a login.

12
00:00:30,348 --> 00:00:35,002
The real thing is a repository manager like nexus or artifactory,

13
00:00:35,002 --> 00:00:37,580
but Maven sees the same four oh one.

14
00:00:39,130 --> 00:00:42,842
A repository is where Maven downloads libraries from.

15
00:00:42,842 --> 00:00:44,383
Your pom can name one,

16
00:00:44,383 --> 00:00:46,063
with a repository block.

17
00:00:46,613 --> 00:00:51,392
A mirror is a detour. Maven asks the mirror, instead of the original address.

18
00:00:51,942 --> 00:00:57,126
Companies use mirrors for speed, for control, and because the mirror can cache everything.

19
00:00:57,676 --> 00:00:59,221
A mirror matches by id.

20
00:00:59,221 --> 00:01:05,335
Mirror of libs means: every request for the repository with the id libs goes to the mirror.

21
00:01:05,885 --> 00:01:07,919
So the pom keeps the public address,

22
00:01:07,919 --> 00:01:11,197
and each developer's settings decide where it really goes.

23
00:01:13,247 --> 00:01:18,175
Two files configure Maven. The pom belongs to the project, and lives in git.

24
00:01:18,725 --> 00:01:21,773
The settings dot X M L file belongs to you.

25
00:01:21,773 --> 00:01:23,829
It lives in your home folder,

26
00:01:23,829 --> 00:01:24,751
in dot m two.

27
00:01:24,751 --> 00:01:25,530
Not in git.

28
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:31,499
Mirrors go here, and so do servers. A server block holds a user name and a password.

29
00:01:32,049 --> 00:01:34,097
The link between them is the id.

30
00:01:34,097 --> 00:01:35,889
The server with the id corp,

31
00:01:35,889 --> 00:01:38,193
matches the mirror with the id corp.

32
00:01:38,743 --> 00:01:44,119
If the ids differ by one letter, Maven sends no password, and you get the four oh one.

33
00:01:46,169 --> 00:01:48,814
Where does the password itself go? Three rules.

34
00:01:49,364 --> 00:01:49,617
One.

35
00:01:49,617 --> 00:01:50,691
Never in the pom,

36
00:01:50,691 --> 00:01:52,523
and never in any file in git.

37
00:01:52,523 --> 00:01:55,935
Anyone who can read the project can read the password.

38
00:01:56,485 --> 00:01:56,782
Two.

39
00:01:56,782 --> 00:01:57,672
In settings,

40
00:01:57,672 --> 00:02:00,121
refer to an environment variable.

41
00:02:00,121 --> 00:02:00,641
Dollar,

42
00:02:00,641 --> 00:02:01,086
brace,

43
00:02:01,086 --> 00:02:01,680
env dot,

44
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:02,348
the name.

45
00:02:02,348 --> 00:02:05,168
The real value stays outside the file.

46
00:02:05,718 --> 00:02:11,392
Three. Lock down the files. C H mod six hundred means: only you can read the file.

47
00:02:11,942 --> 00:02:15,382
Maven can also encrypt passwords with a master password.

48
00:02:15,382 --> 00:02:17,163
It is better than plain text,

49
00:02:17,163 --> 00:02:21,094
but an environment variable from a secret store is better still.

50
00:02:23,144 --> 00:02:26,301
Go to the demo project. Type: CD, demo.

51
00:02:26,851 --> 00:02:32,590
Look at the settings file. Type: cat, tilde, slash, dot m two, slash settings dot X M L.

52
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:33,737
A server,

53
00:02:33,737 --> 00:02:34,864
with the id corp,

54
00:02:34,864 --> 00:02:37,782
and the password as an environment variable.

55
00:02:37,782 --> 00:02:38,644
And a mirror,

56
00:02:38,644 --> 00:02:39,772
with the same id,

57
00:02:39,772 --> 00:02:40,103
corp,

58
00:02:40,103 --> 00:02:41,695
for the repository libs.

59
00:02:42,245 --> 00:02:49,477
Now build, without the password. Type: mvn -B -q clean package, then filter the error.

60
00:02:50,027 --> 00:02:52,245
Maven asks the mirror for the libraries.

61
00:02:58,795 --> 00:03:04,299
Four oh one. Unauthorized. The server exists, but Maven has no password to send.

62
00:03:04,849 --> 00:03:06,379
Give it the password,

63
00:03:06,379 --> 00:03:07,764
from a locked file.

64
00:03:07,764 --> 00:03:10,022
Type: export REPO_PASS= dollar,

65
00:03:10,022 --> 00:03:10,897
parenthesis,

66
00:03:10,897 --> 00:03:13,447
cat tilde slash dot corp dash pass.

67
00:03:13,997 --> 00:03:16,898
Build again. Type: mvn -B -q clean package && ls target slash star dot jar

68
00:03:18,528 --> 00:03:20,469
Now it downloads, with the login.

69
00:03:28,019 --> 00:03:32,777
A jar. The build works, and the password never appeared in a command, or a file.

70
00:03:33,327 --> 00:03:39,492
Prove it. Search every file here for the password.

71
00:03:40,842 --> 00:03:45,258
Nothing found. The password lives only in the variable, and in the locked file.

72
00:03:45,808 --> 00:03:52,187
Last, lock the settings file. Type: chmod 600, then stat, to show the permissions.

73
00:03:53,637 --> 00:03:59,311
Dash, r w, and dashes. Only the owner can read or write. Both files are locked.

74
00:04:00,861 --> 00:04:01,816
Your lab task.

75
00:04:01,816 --> 00:04:05,975
Lab ninety gives you a project and a repository with a login.

76
00:04:05,975 --> 00:04:07,816
Lab check judges your work.

77
00:04:08,366 --> 00:04:10,533
It lives at git hub dot com,

78
00:04:10,533 --> 00:04:12,081
slash it dash waves,

79
00:04:12,081 --> 00:04:13,862
slash dev ops dash A I,

80
00:04:13,862 --> 00:04:14,713
in academy,

81
00:04:14,713 --> 00:04:15,023
lab,

82
00:04:15,023 --> 00:04:16,494
lab ninety dot S H.

83
00:04:17,044 --> 00:04:18,858
One: in settings dot X M L,

84
00:04:18,858 --> 00:04:20,874
add a server with the id corp,

85
00:04:20,874 --> 00:04:21,747
the user dev,

86
00:04:21,747 --> 00:04:25,577
and the password from the environment variable corp pass.

87
00:04:26,127 --> 00:04:28,324
Two: add a mirror with the id corp,

88
00:04:28,324 --> 00:04:29,830
for the repository libs,

89
00:04:29,830 --> 00:04:32,655
with the address on port eight zero nine one.

90
00:04:33,205 --> 00:04:36,149
Three: lock the file with C H mod six hundred.

91
00:04:36,699 --> 00:04:39,812
Four: set the variable from the password file,

92
00:04:39,812 --> 00:04:42,587
and build with dash s settings dot X M L.

93
00:04:42,587 --> 00:04:44,550
It must end in build success.

94
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:47,512
Five: one sentence in why dot text,

95
00:04:47,512 --> 00:04:50,682
about why the password must not be in the pom.

96
00:04:50,682 --> 00:04:51,923
Use the words pom,

97
00:04:51,923 --> 00:04:53,164
and git or commit.

98
00:04:53,714 --> 00:04:56,136
Install a J D K and Maven first.

99
00:04:56,136 --> 00:04:57,044
Then lab up,

100
00:04:57,044 --> 00:04:57,801
lab check,

101
00:04:57,801 --> 00:04:58,785
and lab down.

102
00:04:58,785 --> 00:05:01,661
The shortcut file is shortcuts ninety.

103
00:05:01,661 --> 00:05:03,780
The check downloads plugins,

104
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:04,764
so be online.

105
00:05:06,814 --> 00:05:08,883
Pass or fail. You need all six.

106
00:05:09,433 --> 00:05:11,247
Five pass lines in lab check.

107
00:05:11,797 --> 00:05:14,335
You can explain a mirror, and what mirror of means.

108
00:05:14,885 --> 00:05:18,085
You can explain why server and mirror ids must match.

109
00:05:18,635 --> 00:05:21,622
No password in any file, only in the environment.

110
00:05:22,172 --> 00:05:24,753
The settings file is locked, mode six hundred.

111
00:05:25,303 --> 00:05:27,223
lab down, nothing left behind.

112
00:05:27,773 --> 00:05:29,075
To pass, all six.

113
